steps.body:
An object type lists each property with its type, between braces. A `type` alias gives that shape a name, so you can use it in annotations anywhere:

```typescript
type User = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
};

const ada: User = { id: 1, name: "Ada" };
console.log(`${ada.id}: ${ada.name}`); // 1: Ada
```

Every property is required. An object without `name`, or with a number in `name`, is a type error. In return, wherever a value has the type `User`, TypeScript knows its properties and their types.

You can also write a shape inline, right in a parameter. That suits a shape you use only once:

```typescript
function label(item: { name: string; qty: number }): string {
  return `${item.qty} x ${item.name}`;
}

console.log(label({ name: "Mug", qty: 2 })); // 2 x Mug
```

Shapes nest: a property can hold another object type, and `User[]` is an array of users. Like annotations, aliases are erased before the code runs, so they cost nothing at runtime. Name types in PascalCase, like `User` and `OrderLine`.

examples.code:
type User = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
};

function describe(user: User): string {
  return `#${user.id} ${user.name} <${user.email}>`;
}

const users: User[] = [
  { id: 1, name: "Ada", email: "ada@example.com" },
  { id: 2, name: "Grace", email: "grace@example.com" },
];

for (const user of users) {
  console.log(describe(user));
}

type Address = {
  street: string;
  city: string;
};

type Customer = {
  name: string;
  address: Address;
};

const ada: Customer = {
  name: "Ada",
  address: { street: "12 Byron St", city: "London" },
};

const where = ada.address;
//    ^? const where: Address
console.log(`${ada.name}, ${where.street}, ${where.city}`);

type User = {
  id: number;
  name: string;
};

const user: User = { id: 1 };
console.log(user.id);

