practice.solution:
export type Settings = {
  readonly userId: number;
  theme?: string;
  fontSize?: number;
};

export function describeSettings(settings: Settings): string {
  const theme = settings.theme ?? "light";
  const fontSize = settings.fontSize ?? 16;
  return `user ${settings.userId}: ${theme} theme, ${fontSize}px`;
}

const mine: Settings = { userId: 7, theme: "dark" };
const yours: Settings = { userId: 8, fontSize: 20 };

console.log(describeSettings(mine));
console.log(describeSettings(yours));

steps.body:
TypeScript compares shapes, not names. A value fits a type when it has every property the type needs, with the right types, whatever the type is called:

```typescript
type Point = { x: number; y: number };
type Size = { x: number; y: number };

const screen: Size = { x: 640, y: 480 };
const corner: Point = screen;
console.log(`${corner.x} by ${corner.y}`); // 640 by 480
```

Extra properties are fine too, since a value with more can still do everything the type needs. This is called structural typing.

There is one exception. An object literal written straight into a typed place, like an annotated variable or a function argument, may not have properties the type lacks. TypeScript reports each one as an excess property (TS2353), because an extra key in a fresh literal is almost always a typo. When the key is close to a real one, the message suggests it (TS2561).

So pass options as a literal, or annotate the variable that holds them, and the checker catches `colour` where you meant `color`. A literal stored in an unannotated variable first gets no such check.

examples.code:
type Point = {
  x: number;
  y: number;
};

function printPoint(point: Point): void {
  console.log(`(${point.x}, ${point.y})`);
}

const corner = { x: 4, y: 7 };
const spot = { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 };
//    ^? const spot: { x: number; y: number; z: number; }

printPoint(corner);
printPoint(spot);

type Point = {
  x: number;
  y: number;
};

const p: Point = { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 };
console.log(`(${p.x}, ${p.y})`);

type NewUser = {
  name: string;
  email?: string;
};

function createUser(options: NewUser): string {
  return `${options.name} <${options.email ?? "no email"}>`;
}

console.log(createUser({ name: "Ada", emial: "ada@example.com" }));

