practice.solution:
export type QuoteOptions = {
  weightKg: number;
  express?: boolean;
  insured?: boolean;
};

export function quote(options: QuoteOptions): number {
  const base = 500 + 200 * options.weightKg;
  const express = options.express ? 700 : 0;
  const insured = options.insured ? 150 : 0;
  return base + express + insured;
}

console.log(`2 kg: ${quote({ weightKg: 2 })} cents`);
console.log(`2 kg express: ${quote({ weightKg: 2, express: true })} cents`);
console.log(`5 kg express insured: ${quote({ weightKg: 5, express: true, insured: true })} cents`);

steps.body:
Some objects are dictionaries: any number of keys, all holding the same type of value. `Record<string, number>` is the type of such an object, with string keys and number values. An index signature, `{ [key: string]: number }`, says the same thing in another form.

```typescript
const stock: Record<string, number> = { apple: 3, pear: 0 };
stock["kiwi"] = 12;
console.log(Object.keys(stock).join(", ")); // apple, pear, kiwi
```

Any key may be missing, so with this course's `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, reading one gives `number | undefined`. Handle the missing case with `??` or a check before you use the value:

```typescript
const stock: Record<string, number> = { apple: 3, pear: 0 };
const kiwis = stock["kiwi"];
//    ^? const kiwis: number | undefined
console.log(kiwis ?? 0); // 0
```

A `Map<string, number>` does the same job, and its `get` also returns `number | undefined`. Prefer a `Map` when keys come and go while the program runs, or come from input: it has a `size`, keeps keys in the exact order you add them, and has no inherited keys like `toString`. A `Record` is plain data, so `JSON.stringify` prints it as is.

examples.code:
const stock: Record<string, number> = { apple: 3, pear: 0 };

function inStock(fruit: string): string {
  const count = stock[fruit] ?? 0;
  return count > 0 ? `${fruit}: ${count} left` : `${fruit}: sold out`;
}

stock["kiwi"] = 12;

console.log(inStock("apple"));
console.log(inStock("pear"));
console.log(inStock("kiwi"));
console.log(inStock("mango"));

const sentence = "the cat and the hat and the bat";
const counts: { [word: string]: number } = {};

for (const word of sentence.split(" ")) {
  counts[word] = (counts[word] ?? 0) + 1;
}

for (const [word, count] of Object.entries(counts)) {
  console.log(`${word}: ${count}`);
}

examples.explanation:
`{ [word: string]: number }` is the same type as `Record<string, number>`, written as an index signature. The name `word` is only a label for the key. The first time a word appears, `counts[word]` is missing, so `?? 0` starts it at 0 before adding 1. `Object.entries` gives `[string, number]` pairs in the order the keys were added. Leave out the `??` and the checker stops the program, since the value may be `undefined`:

```typescript error
const counts: { [word: string]: number } = {};
counts["the"] = counts["the"] + 1;
```

examples.code:
const sentence = "the cat and the hat and the bat";
const counts = new Map<string, number>();

for (const word of sentence.split(" ")) {
  counts.set(word, (counts.get(word) ?? 0) + 1);
}

const the = counts.get("the");
//    ^? const the: number | undefined
console.log(`the: ${the ?? 0}, words: ${counts.size}`);

const sorted = [...counts].sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
console.log(sorted.map(([word, count]) => `${word} ${count}`).join(", "));

